CORRECTION to my b11 report (important): I said a Windows touchpad is animated in Chromium. That is only the legacy path. I have now read more source:
- content/browser/renderer_host/direct_manipulation_helper_win.cc configures Windows DirectManipulation with TRANSLATION_X/Y, TRANSLATION_INERTIA, RAILS and SCALING. So on a Windows precision touchpad, Chromium does NOT use WM_MOUSEWHEEL. Scrolling and pinch come from DirectManipulation.
- ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc: ApplyPanGestureScroll / ApplyPanGestureFling / FlingBegin/End build a ui::ScrollEvent with momentum phases (INERTIAL_UPDATE etc.), and ApplyPinchZoomScale builds a GesturePinchUpdate with the touchpad scale.
- ui/events/blink/web_input_event.cc: ScrollEvent becomes a wheel event with kScrollByPrecisePixel, so cc/input/input_handler.cc ShouldAnimateScroll is false. Touchpad scroll is applied directly, with no easing curve.
- That answers the user's question: the inertia he feels in Edge is Windows' own DirectManipulation inertia, delivered to Chromium as momentum-phase events. Chromium does not compute the fling curve itself in these files. The same DirectManipulation path gives Edge a continuous pinch.
- LitePDF uses winit. winit 0.30 (platform_impl/windows/event_loop.rs) handles only WM_MOUSEWHEEL and sends LineDelta = delta/120. It does not use DirectManipulation, so it gets neither the OS inertia nor continuous pinch. Whether Windows adds an inertia tail as extra WM_MOUSEWHEEL events is unknown. The recorder will show it.
- The cc scroll curve (b11) is still the right copy for mouse wheel and non-precision devices.

WHAT b12 DOES
1. Recorder: Diagnostics -> "Record input events to input.log" (in %APPDATA%\LitePDF next to perf.log). It logs every wheel event (unit, dx, dy, ctrl/shift/alt), zoom and touch events with a ms timestamp. It logs every active frame with dt, scroll applied (split into touchpad, wheel animation and momentum), scroll offset, displayed zoom, target zoom and momentum velocity. After a swipe or pinch it shows whether events keep arriving after the fingers lift.
2. Zoom, cause of the jumps: each ctrl+wheel event added a whole x1.25 step (clamp of Chromium's gesture_detector.ts) and b11 smoothed it over only ~35 ms, about 2 frames. He saw 166 -> 207 (x1.247). b12: events only move a TARGET zoom. The displayed zoom follows it every frame in log space (exponential approach, tau 50 ms, capped at 3.5 ln-units/s, both scaled by frame time), anchored at the cursor, starting on the first frame. The function is zoom_step() in app.rs. Headless proof (native test of that exact function, /tmp/zt.rs): a single x1.25 step 166->207 changes at most 6.0% per frame at 60 Hz and 2.5% per frame at 144 Hz, over 25 and 57 frames. The first 144 Hz frame goes 1.66 -> 1.7008 (+2.46%). A 10-step pinch and a 50-event stream stay under the same caps. The real app path through the event loop is verified only by that function test and by Wine uitest running (pinch frame max 2.6 ms); the feel is PC-only.
3. Scroll: the code was reading LineDelta correctly (winit on Windows always gives LineDelta = units/120; egui-winit passes it as Line, no pixel conversion), so I found no unit bug. One verified difference from Chromium: legacy builder multiplies by SPI_GETWHEELSCROLLLINES (system setting) times 100/3 px, and I hard-coded 3. b12 now reads the system setting as Chromium does (sys_lines() in app.rs). I could not verify the cause of "too fast". I cannot measure it without his log. The two real candidates were the b10 deviations (fixed in b11) and touchpad deltas being treated as discrete wheel ticks that were animated and retargeted. b12 changes that: events with fine or fractional line values (touchpad) are applied directly, like Chromium's precise path. Integer-line values (mouse notches) still use the Chromium animation curve. If it is still too fast, input.log will show the deltas per swipe, and the Scroll distance slider in Diagnostics is the immediate knob.
4. Inertia: because winit can't give LitePDF the OS's DirectManipulation inertia, this is MY approximation, not Chromium's: when touchpad events stop for 45 ms, scrolling continues at the last measured velocity (smoothed over events) and decays exponentially with tau 325 ms. It starts the frame after the stream stops, any new event cancels it, and it stops under 120 px/s. If Windows already sends its own inertial wheel events, the 45 ms gap means my momentum only takes over the residual tail. The tau/threshold values are guesses and the log will tune them.
Not changed: tiles, tabs, page selector, overlay, render fix.

